<p>Chapter IV runs from printed page 29 through page 39 and holds the last eight stories: bar characters, marital quarrels, a hunting-camp radio joke, two Minnesota boys on the road to Creede, and a closing essay on flatulence that the author calls a "fitting finale to this publication." Three more pages follow the last story and are not listed in the table of contents: a photograph and verse headed "One More Clean Shirt for Old Robert" (p. 40), a photograph and verse for Victor Miller and Noel Oates, and the back cover with a paragraph about the author. All three are transcribed at the end of this page.</p>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="ill-drink-to-that-or-anything-else">I'll Drink to That (Or Anything Else)</h2>

  <p>A young fellow in a newspaper office penned a memo to his superior that my articles were mainly about alcohol and drinking. He was right and I apologize for spending an inordinate amount of time writing about drunks, but like our beloved Will Rogers who never met a man he didn't like, I seldom met a drunk I didn't like. In mining you face a certain amount of danger as soon as you enter the hole. We depended on a narrow strand of cable spooling from small tugger hoists to lower us safely in winzes and shafts as we stood on the rims of the buckets and, again, to pull us safely to the haulage tunnel at the end of our shift. We lit our fuse to the primers and dynamite with either our carbide lamps or a length of fuse notched to spit fire every inch. When lighting as many as 50 ends hanging from holes in a shrink stope and allowing 5 seconds for blasting interval there was little time to get the hell out of there when the last fuse hissed with flame and smoke. With seven hours behind you holding a tight cinch on your pucker string it was a great relief to gather in a local bar with fellow miners and down a boilermaker or two to unwind. Laughter is one of our most pleasing sounds and there is much laughter in bars. There are characters who wouldn't give you the time of day until they wet their whistle with 86 proof then would astound us with knowledge and tales from other camps. Frustrated singers, actors, orators and dancers lived a few hours of their fantasies during our gatherings of drinking and camaraderie.</p>

  <figure class="source-doc-image">
    <img src="/images/site/who-upset-bar-crew-ray-ball.jpg" alt="Sepia photograph of five men along a bar, taken from the end of the counter: three seated men in hats or work shirts face the camera, a fourth sits in shadow behind them, and a bartender in a white shirt stands at right holding a bottle. Glasses, a napkin holder and bottles crowd the bar top.">
    <figcaption>L to R: Ray Ball, Pete Walker, Lucky Keene, Archie "Spud" Fellows,<br>Bartender - Ed Hargraves</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="source-doc-poem">
    <p>"Bottom's up, here's mud in your eye,<br>
    and pour us another, your turn to buy."</p>
  </div>

  <p>Women, as rule, don't make good two fisted drinkers. Ruby, a tall, skinny redhead came as close as any to joining our fraternity. The old girl could slug it down like a pro and had the courtesy to excuse herself before kneeling over to sleep it off. She had long fingernails and the constant clicking of her nails on the glasses irritated the hell out of one of our elders. Unc Hood would begin glaring at her after the second drink and retire to a corner table until she left or passed out. We liked Ruby. Most of the females would begin blubbering about a worthless husband or unconcerned boyfriend after three or four drinks, but Ruby had no inner turmoil. She could sing bawdy songs and rip off a joke with the best of the males. We were beginning to think she had all the necessary credentials. Then one night she miscalculated her capacity and puked all over the bar. She withdrew her application and shortly thereafter moved the California.</p>

  <p>During one summer we were joined by an elderly gentleman who had leased a barber shop and was a regular at the bar after working hours. Winston liked to be addressed by his first name, but he referred to us as "Mister Jackson" or "Mister Hutchinson" or Mister whoever he was talking to. He was a very learned man, loved poetry and could recite with a flair normally reserved for someone on stage. As time went on Winston began spending more time at the bar than at his shop and business dropped off. He asked me if his haircuts were not suitable or if his patrons were going to the valley barbers. I told him the problem was he might be drinking too much. "Mister Jackson, I resent that statement. I have never drank too much, I invariably pass out before I have had my fill."</p>

  <p>Another evening, as I slid onto a barstool beside him he looked sorrowfully at me and whispered, "I shall not be able to drink with you tonight, Mister Jackson." I was afraid I had offended him and asked if he was mad at me.</p>

  <p>"Oh no, not at all, sir, but I believe I am either dead or very near death's door."</p>

  <p>"Come on, Winston, you look pretty chipper to me." I responded.</p>

  <p>"Mister Jackson, I recently read where the last act of an expiring body is to purge itself of all wastes, liquid or solid and I fear I have done both without moving from my stool."</p>

  <p>By the aroma I felt he was right and I left.</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>The photograph and its couplet fill the lower half of printed page 29, below the opening paragraph. The caption names five men and does not name the bar. Raymond Ball is named beside Hank Slaght in the Amethyst crew photograph in <a href="/writings/please-go-to-hell/dedication-and-the-amethyst-crew-1947/">"Please Go to Hell, John Jackson"</a> and appears as "Ray Ball" in a 1940 photograph caption in the "Memories" section of <a href="/writings/echoes-from-the-thundermug/memories/">"Echoes from the Thundermug."</a> Pete Walker "who hauled our ore" is in Echoes, and Chapter II of this book gives his name as Ralph "Pete" Walker. Archie "Spud" Fellows is the full-time moonshiner of <a href="/writings/please-go-to-hell/hellions-and-moonshiners/">"Hellions and Moonshiners."</a> An Ed Hargraves is named in Echoes, in <a href="/writings/echoes-from-the-thundermug/grown-up-in-creede-part-three/#talking-to-yourself-ok-but-dont-start-believing-it">"Talking to Yourself OK, But Don't Start Believing It,"</a> as a man who talks to himself; that page gives no occupation, and no other page gives him one. Lucky Keene is not named anywhere else on the site. "Unc Hood" matches Clyde "Unc" Hood, the railroad engineer with the old army overcoat, who is described in Echoes in <a href="/writings/echoes-from-the-thundermug/grown-up-in-creede-part-one/#a-view-from-the-barroom-floor">"A View from the Barroom Floor"</a> and captioned in "Memories" as "R.R. Engineer, Gentle Spirit." Ruby and Winston are not named anywhere else on the site, and the story does not say which Creede bar this was. "Moved the California" and "Women, as rule" are as printed.</em></p>
</section>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="let-me-put-it-another-way">Let Me Put It Another Way</h2>

  <p>Any young rooster can chase a pullet across the barnyard, but it takes an old cock to really make a hen ruffle her feathers.</p>

  <p>A couple attended a dance in Creede. They were a good looking pair, danced well and were enjoying the evening to the fullest until Elmer came up missing. His wife asked me if I had seen him. I told her I had, indeed, seen him without adding any pertinent details except that I was sure he would return. She said she was going home, she was tired and sleepy, and if I saw him again to tell him where she had gone.</p>

  <p>When she arrived home she found Elmer in bed with a young lass who liked the feel of different sheets. Bathed in the light of a 100 watt bulb Elmer rose, looked first at his wife, then at his bed partner and cried, "Oh my God, I was so drunk I though it was you, honest honey." She didn't believe him.</p>

  <p>I was socializing with a splendid group in the Elk's bar one dance night. I was with both locals and old friends who had been away for years in other states. With all the toasts and rounds to the departed and those in danger of departing, I became severely overloaded and began feeling my way around. A perturbed husband and Inez arrived at the same time. I told her that due to my condition I wasn't seeing well, but I knew her dimensions to the very centimeter and was trying to find her so she could take me home. She believed me not at all and the husband believed me even less.</p>

  <p>In view of those two incidents, I would advise every young male to try, with all his heart and soul, to get along with his Mother. She will be the last female he will know who will listen to his side of the story.</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>The epigraph under the title is printed on its own line, as it is here. The book gives Elmer no surname and does not name his wife or the woman in the bed. "I though it was you" and "Elk's bar" are as printed. Inez is <a href="/people/john-richard-jackson/#inez-mae-schriver">Inez Mae Schriver</a>, John's wife. The closing advice is repeated, in slightly different words, in the last paragraph of "I Didn't Hear It 'Til It Was Already Passed," below.</em></p>
</section>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="records-were-made-to-be-broken">Records Were Made To Be Broken</h2>

  <p>If we pass 50 years of age without a string of successes we can be fairly certain we will never be listed in "Who's Who" or the Guinness Book of Records. If we pass 65 years of age without a single success we should accept the fact we are doomed to obscurity. I begged for recognition when I was mucking out tons upon tons of blasted rock followed by setting up a Leyner "Wiggle Tail" drill to riddle the face with holes and blast out another smoking pile of rock. Fame didn't come until the day I got loop-legged drunk and ran over several 55 gallon barrels behind Tomkin's Hardware Store. A couple of crushed barrels got hung up on the chassis of the 1948 Chevrolet truck and veered me into a large snowbank further along the route home. As I struggled with the dilemma I was recognized by 317 of the 364 residents of Creede, Colorado. The town marshal, the principal of the grade school, the new Preacher of the Community Church and my bride were in the audience.</p>

  <p>I drank more than my digestive tract could safely handle during those years of adjusting to a new and strange lifestyle. Marriage had problems that I was unable to solve with a pair of fists or a swearing, furniture throwing tantrum. The little gal from Kansas was a good scrapper and had both temper and stubbornness to match her teutonic ancestors. We lived, briefly, in a one room cabin with a lumpy bed, a wood cookstove, outside water and a two holer 30 yards to the east. There was never a complaint over living conditions, but there was hell to pay if I came home with a load of barley corn under my belt. I had a good head of hair then with a wave in the front. She would grab me by the forelocks, drop my head to where she could connect with her other hand and draw blood from my nose. I couldn't believe she would get that riled up so early in our marriage. For a measure of defense I had my hair cut flat-top, a style I have worn since though she has lost her timing and much of the force behind her punches.</p>

  <p>Before I get into other things I should mention that my bride had a traditional wedding shower by a group of ladies in Creede. The shower was held in what had served as the Red Cross Hall and training quarters for aspiring boxers. I thought it a fitting setting. I was summoned to help in cutting a large cake at the height of the ceremony. I was standing, self-consciously, to one side when an elderly matron, a family friend, sidled up to me and whispered, "The first baby only takes six months, Johnny, all the rest will take nine months." The poor lady went to her reward still wondering.</p>

  <p>I had a friend in our United States Army who held a record of sorts in joint tenancy with his wife. Shortly after they were married he was called up for military duty and then shipped to the Pacific Theater of War. He was gone for 21 months. Three months after his return to the home fires his wife presented him with a normal weight male heir. It was either the longest or the shortest human gestation period on record though I can't remember if either claimed the honors.</p>

  <p>I also knew a lady who gave birth nearly two years after her husband died. It was presumed the cold weather of Creede held the deceased's sperm immobile until she got too near the pot bellied stove in a beer parlor...or another location just as warm.</p>

  <figure class="source-doc-image source-doc-image--compact">
    <img src="/images/site/who-upset-inez-and-skeeter.jpg" alt="Sepia photograph of a smiling woman with short curly hair, wearing a quilted hooded jacket, hugging a husky-type dog with a dark-and-white face against her chest.">
    <figcaption>Inez M. Jackson and Skeeter</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="source-doc-poem">
    <p>Life has no meaning without a love or a pal<br>
    and I have had both in this smiling gal.</p>

    <p>Our cup runneth over, each day so fulfilling<br>
    and another tomorrow if the good Lord is willing.</p>
  </div>

  <p>I believe science must be baffled by the records being set in this generation. If they don't slack off with genetic engineering the baby will be born before the gentleman can even tip his hat and when there ain't no fun in it people will soon lose interest.</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>The photograph of Inez and Skeeter fills the lower part of printed page 32, between "immobile" (the last word of the text on that page) and "until" (the first word on page 33), so the sentence about the pot bellied stove runs across it. It is set here after the paragraph that contains the break, so the paragraph reads whole. "My bride" and "the little gal from Kansas" are Inez, whose page records her birth in Melrose, Kansas. Echoes has a husky named Skeeter who works the ranch and goes prospecting with John, in <a href="/writings/echoes-from-the-thundermug/married-life-and-mining-camps-part-four/#dog-a-big-help-on-prospecting-expedition">"Dog a Big Help on Prospecting Expedition"</a> and <a href="/writings/echoes-from-the-thundermug/married-life-and-mining-camps-part-four/#anythings-better-than-being-alone">"Anything's Better Than Being Alone."</a> The marshal and the barrels turn up in two other places with different details. Echoes, in <a href="/writings/echoes-from-the-thundermug/grown-up-in-creede-part-one/#sweet-beetles">"Sweet Beetles,"</a> has John "pushing 55-gallon barrels through snow banks with a Chevrolet truck" until Marshal Bill Kolisch turns him over to his bride, and <a href="/writings/please-go-to-hell/the-price-of-glory/">"The Price of Glory"</a> has a single barrel wrapped around the front axle and pushed seven blocks. This version puts the barrels behind Tomkin's Hardware, dates the truck to 1948, and names no marshal. Echoes spells the store "Tomkins Hardware Co." in <a href="/writings/echoes-from-the-thundermug/creede-boyhood-part-one/#stealing-aint-as-easy-as-it-was-in-times-past">"Stealing Ain't as Easy as It Was in Times Past."</a> The bridal shower in the old Red Cross Hall is told again in Echoes, in <a href="/writings/echoes-from-the-thundermug/married-life-and-mining-camps-part-two/#wedding-reminds-us-of-our-own">"Wedding Reminds Us of Our Own,"</a> where the matron's remark ("The first baby only takes 6 months, but all the others will take nine") is followed by "We fooled her" and Gavin Skinner brings John from the Rainbow Bar; here it is addressed to "Johnny" and ends with the lady "still wondering." The Red Cross Hall as the boxers' training quarters is in "Once Around the Thunder Mug," <a href="/writings/once-around-the-thunder-mug/of-bagpipes-coasting-and-firemen/#lessons-from-a-barbers-chair">"Lessons from a Barber's Chair."</a> John's wedding itself is told in Chapter III, in <a href="/writings/who-upset-the-thundermug/chapter-iii/#wedding-bells-and-chesapeake-retrievers">"Wedding Bells and Chesapeake Retrievers."</a> "Teutonic" is as printed. The matron and the two friends in the anecdotes that follow are not named.</em></p>
