<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="you-read-it-here-first">You Read It Here First</h2>

  <p>One of life's greatest ironies is to struggle so hard to reach maturity only to find that it is occupied by old people.</p>

  <p>There are two sides to every question and then there's the ladies side...which doesn't fit endways or sideways.</p>

  <p>Ignorance is a valid excuse whereas stupidity straddles a narrow line both in social acceptance or legal matters. I don't believe, in good conscience, we could hang a man for being ignorant; however, I have seen stupid men the noose would fit well. There are many congressmen who would plead ignorance if called in for appraisal by a higher authority. I believe a higher authority would dispatch them to a much lower authority and rightfully so.</p>

  <p>We think we have seen and heard everything vile and revolting then our nation brings forth a new rock group or pseudo entertainers with their brains located between their rectums and genitals where, steeped in cocaine or other drugs, they cast a spell on the young and continue to devalue every facet of human life.</p>

  <p>One of my first goals was to reach an age where I could legally drink alcoholic beverages then the bars raised the price of whiskey to 35&cent; a shot. It took no less than 8 shots to get brave enough to pick a fight with my wife. That amounted to two dollars and eighty cents which was a great deal of money to pay to get the hell beat out of me.</p>

  <p>I was chauffeuring a friend who was engaged in some sort of ritual with a young lady in the back seat. The rear view became more interesting than the front view whereupon our vehicle entered a ditch, became airborne for several feet before slewing sideways across the road to the other ditch where it righted itself and we continued with no more motoring difficulties. I was pleased to note that the figures in the back seat were not dislodged. This leads me to believe that a firm anchor will control the most wayward ship.</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>This story is a run of separate aphorisms and anecdotes; the first begins directly under the heading, as printed. The friend and the young lady in the last anecdote are not named.</em></p>
</section>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="purse-your-lips-before-you-blow">Purse Your Lips Before You Blow</h2>

  <p>There are two kinds of fools in this world. Those who are born to be fools and those who work at it, Mrs. Jackson believes I am both.</p>

  <p>If I live to be as old as Methuselah I will never understand women. My bride was trying to lead me to the backdoor of our home when I suddenly became violently ill. "Just look at you," she cried, "if you aren't a sorry sight, throwing up out here where the whole world can see you."</p>

  <p>Not long after that, following another meeting of the Upper Willow Creek Mineralogical Society and Glee Club, I made it into the house and nearly to the bathroom before I parked my cookies and she still scolded me.</p>

  <p>I had a good friend who was an alcoholic. He told me his wife drove him to drink. I, too, am an alcoholic, but unlike the experience of my friend, my wife tried to drive me from drink. I believe everyone will agree she was a poor driver.</p>

  <p>At this late date the old girl seems unduly concerned with the amount of grey hair accumulating in my ears. We have chairs on opposite sides of a small stand that contains books as well as supporting a lamp. At least twice monthly I can see her peering over her bi-focals at my left ear. If I don't hurry outdoors she will have her little manicure scissors snipping away drawing blood.</p>

  <p>I like that hair. If you have ever had a gnat or other small insect take up quarters in your ear drum you can appreciate the comfort a growth like that affords by giving you time to mash the culprit before it can start doing barrel rolls in the inner ear.</p>

  <p>I was in a bar one night where a gentle person kept wanting to blow in my ear as he rolled his eyes in mind boggling revolutions. Fact is, he wanted to blow in everyone's ears so I wasn't singled out. There are a few of us left of that crew and we all seem to have hairy ears. I now wonder if that poor fellow had anything to do with it. Probably not, but there's a lot of body chemistry we know nothing about. Next time someone blows in your ear check it out for a few days and let me know if anything sprouts.</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>"Mrs. Jackson" and "my bride" are Inez. The "Upper Willow Creek Mineralogical Society" is the same jokingly named club Echoes places in the rear of Tomkin's Hardware, where its "first annual meeting" was also its last; in <a href="/writings/echoes-from-the-thundermug/grown-up-in-creede-part-two/#women-can-scare-strongest-man">"Women Can Scare Strongest Man"</a> Mother Bee McClure blasts it "to smithereens," and in <a href="/writings/echoes-from-the-thundermug/married-life-and-mining-camps-part-three/#mother-bees-tantrum-put-an-end-to-party">"Mother Bee's Tantrum Put an End to Party"</a> she disbands it and the "Tomkins Hardware Brotherhood of Mine and Mill Suppliers" together. "And Glee Club" is added only here.</em></p>
</section>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="forest-blows-a-10-4">Forest Blows a 10-4</h2>

  <p>Gene Wardell and I were driving a tunnel near a ranch owned by Forest Cadwell of Cathedral. It is an area of deep canyons, heavily forested with both spruce and aspen on either side of Spring Creek.</p>

  <p>As a sideline to his ranching Forest had a clientele of hunters from the Fremont County and La Junta areas. Among the group was an old friend, Leo Lockhart, who arrived prior to opening of elk season with a pair of Walky Talkys, hand held radios, he had purchased thinking they would prove of value in keeping track of each other in the wilderness.</p>

  <p>They were in upper Spring Creek with Forest at a high vantage point where he was supposed to meet Leo before hunting another basin. They had been bantering back and forth with the radios and Leo was within a hundred yards of Forest when a single engine airplane passed over Cascade Mountain.</p>

  <p>Forest pushed the button and said, "Hello little airplane, how's the weather up there?"</p>

  <p>Leo heard it clearly and seized the opportunity. He stuffed the radio he carried under his jacket and disguising his voice, answered, "This is NC one...double zero...four...four...nine, who am I talking to?"</p>

  <p>Forest nearly jumped out of his skin. It took him a moment to recover, but he again pressed the button and looking at the plane directly overhead added, "My name's Forest Cadwell and I'm hunting elk on the mountain just under you."</p>

  <p>Leo continued his side of the conversation, "Well, I don't see you, but good luck hunting, over and out."</p>

  <figure class="source-doc-image">
    <img src="/images/site/who-upset-walky-talky-man.jpg" alt="Black-and-white close photograph, heavily contrasted, of a man in a light bucket hat and a collared work shirt, smiling slightly, his face in shadow under the brim.">
    <figcaption>The Walky Talky Man on the Right Wave Length</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="source-doc-poem">
    <p>My friend talks to airplanes, he says they talk back<br>
    I tried it myself, but I don't have the knack.<br>
    So we coddle old Forest, he's on the right track<br>
    when he talks to himself some darned fool answers back.</p>
  </div>

  <p>Leo waited several minutes then went huffing and puffing to Forest's location where he found him beside himself with excitement.</p>

  <p>"Hey, Leo, did you see the airplane that just went by?"</p>

  <p>"Yeah, I heard it then I saw it as it went over San Luis, what about it?"</p>

  <p>"I talked to the pilot," Forest responded.</p>

  <p>"Hell you did! That was a long way to holler."</p>

  <p>"I didn't holler, I talked to him on this radio."</p>

  <p>Forest then quoted their conversation word for word.</p>

  <p>"Aw hell, Forest, there's no way you could have talked to the pilot, these radios aren't on the same frequency as airplanes."</p>

  <p>"I don't know a damned thing about frequencies, but I talked to that pilot, he even told me where he was going."</p>

  <p>Leo shook his head.</p>

  <p>Forest repeated the radio phenomenon to everyone who came in visiting and hunting. It was near the last of the season and Forest was again rehashing his contact with the pilot when Leo covered his mouth with one side of his jacket and chimed in, "This is NC one...double zero...four...four...nine," and doubled up in laughter. Forest couldn't believe it.</p>

  <p>"You no good flimsy excuse for a hunter Lockhart, you've let me make a darned fool of myself all season."</p>

  <p>"If I didn't like your wife so much I'd kill you right here and now."</p>

  <p>That was nearly 30 years ago and they are still friends, but Forest hasn't played with a Walky Talky since.</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>The photograph and couplet fill the middle of printed page 35, between "over and out" and "Leo waited"; they are set in the same place here. The caption is a title, not a name, and the book does not say whom the photograph shows. The verse is about Forest, so the picture may be of him, but that is an inference. Cadwell's ranch on Spring Creek at Cathedral, with its cabins of summer guests, is described in "Echoes from the Thundermug," in <a href="/writings/echoes-from-the-thundermug/married-life-and-mining-camps-part-two/#sore-toe-a-lesson-to-prospector">"Sore Toe a Lesson to Prospector,"</a> and "Forest and Curt Cadwell" are named in <a href="/writings/echoes-from-the-thundermug/married-life-and-mining-camps-part-three/#dog-is-the-dogies-best-friend-as-well-as-masters">"Dog Is the Dogies' Best Friend, as Well as Master's."</a> Gene Wardell is named in many Echoes columns; in <a href="/writings/echoes-from-the-thundermug/married-life-and-mining-camps-part-three/#advice-for-bea-jean">"Advice for Bea Jean"</a> he and John prospected the Mineral Creek headwaters and staked claims. Leo Lockhart is not named elsewhere on the site. The radio call sign is printed with ellipses and no spaces, as here.</em></p>
</section>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="well-i-wiped-it-off-on-my-apron">Well! I Wiped It Off On My Apron</h2>

  <p>The great depression of the '30's was on creating hardship nation-wide when two Minnesota youths learned from a friend of a friend in Colorado that a mining company in the little town of Creede was hiring. They had no money and no transportation, but they were adventurous, used to hard work, and had no qualms about starting a journey to a new life.</p>

  <p>They caught rides, when available, in every manner of vehicle, but mostly they walked. They tried to arrive at small towns or ranches by nightfall for shelter and a chance to exchange labor for meals and lodging. It worked well and they were nearing a halfway point when they detoured from the main road to a farm in Nebraska.</p>

  <p>They approached a middle aged man herding several Holstein cows to a corral and helped him thread them through a gate before introducing themselves. After the initial greetings, Ikie and Bob told the gentleman they were headed for Colorado and would do chores in return for a bed for the night and a couple of meals. Their offer was taken with no hesitation and the farmer outlined work for them. Bob followed him with pails to several stalls for the milking phase as Ikie fed the other animals before starting in on a woodpile with a double-bitted axe.</p>

  <p>They reveled in their stroke of good luck. The evening meal had everything they had been missing for nearly a month and they slept in frame beds with quilts over and under.</p>

  <p>The farmer woke them early. Once again Bob helped with milking as Ikie took over the other chores. He finished before the milkers and gathered up an armload of wood to deposit in the woodbox of the kitchen where the farmer's wife had a cup of freshly perked coffee waiting on the table. She was a hefty, buxom woman and appeared capable of handling any job connected with farming. A pancake griddle was well greased beside two large cast iron skillets in which she had spread thick slices of home-cured bacon. As she turned the bacon and ladled pancake batter on the griddle, a toddler, just able to walk, appeared in the bedroom doorway. He was clad in a pansy flour sack diaper that swayed merrily, side to side, as he waddled over to grasp his mother around one leg. Ikie said he should have known the diaper was loaded.</p>

  <p>The cook took note of her offspring and just as she reached down to pat him, a long, firm turd from the previous night's production slipped from one side of the diaper and clunked on the floor. With no hint of embarrassment or pause in conversation, she worked the spatula under the offender and flipped it into the woodbox. Ikie couldn't believe his eyes!</p>

  <figure class="source-doc-image source-doc-image--compact">
    <img src="/images/site/who-upset-i-d-crawford.jpg" alt="Black-and-white photograph of a man in a dark work jacket over an open-collared shirt, standing with his hands clasped in front of him beside a utility pole on a town street, with buildings and parked cars blurred behind him.">
    <figcaption>"I. D." Crawford</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="source-doc-poem">
    <p>The depression was on andhe needed a job,<br>
    hiked from Minnesota with his good friend, Bob<br>
    hired on as a mucker at three dollars a day<br>
    and soon became foreman<br>
    at a higher rate of pay.</p>
  </div>

  <p>As soon as the farmer and Bob cleared the kitchen door Ikie grabbed Bob by the shoulder.</p>

  <p>"I think I hear a car coming Bob, if we hurry we can catch it."</p>

  <p>Bob looked at Ikie like he thought he had lost his marbles. "I'm not going to miss a meal like this, besides if a car was coming we could see the dust." he replied.</p>

  <p>As Bob walked over to a wash basin and began scrubbing his hands Ikie excused himself, said he wasn't feeling well and he would be on his way. "I'll see you on down the road." he advised Bob.</p>

  <p>When Bob caught up with him Ikie told him of the double duty of the spatula and that was the reason he left so hurriedly. For the next two miles, between retchings, Bob alternately cursed Ikie and the loose boweled kid.</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>On printed page 37 the photograph and its verse sit at the right of the text, beside the kitchen scene, with the paragraphs wrapped around them; they are set here after the paragraph that ends "Ikie couldn't believe his eyes!" The story never says that Ikie is "I. D." Crawford. The caption does: its verse tells the same journey from Minnesota with a friend named Bob, so the book treats them as one man. "Andhe" is printed that way in the caption. Isaac D. Crawford is the superintendent and top miner of the Amethyst crew in <a href="/writings/please-go-to-hell/dedication-and-the-amethyst-crew-1947/">"Please Go to Hell, John Jackson"</a> and is a supervisor in Echoes; the "Ikie" nickname and the friend Bob are not found elsewhere on the site, and the farmer, his wife and the child are not named. Pansy flour-sack diapers also appear in Echoes, in <a href="/writings/echoes-from-the-thundermug/creede-boyhood-part-one/#the-magic-age-is-five">"The Magic Age Is Five."</a> The two closing quotations end in a period before "he replied" and "he advised Bob," as printed.</em></p>
</section>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="i-didnt-hear-it-til-it-was-already-passed">I Didn't Hear It 'Til It Was Already Passed</h2>

  <p>There is no sterile approach to the problem of flatulence or "genteel fahting" as it would be called in New England and I'm not totally convinced it is a problem. One can either stand with a self satisfied smirk on his face and fan the rear of his trousers or point an indignant finger at an innocent bystander while holding his nose with the other hand. This is a controversial topic, but we have never backed away from controversy and it will serve as fitting finale to this publication. I have used the masculine gender because my bride advises me that ladies do not suffer from generation of methane gas in their colons. If she is correct I would judge that 99.9 percent of women are not ladies. I was near a well dressed woman in a supermarket who produced an audible, as well as the unmistakable odor of colonic disturbance. She smiled and I tipped my cap in respect. I had to stifle a, "Bravo, Bravo!"</p>

  <p>Cattle are being maligned as contributors to the expanding hole in the ozone layer and a warming of our climate. This is unfair to both ranchers and their herds. Humans expel more methane gas in one family picnic than I have ever smelled in a barnyard or pasture. I knew an old fellow named Clyde who always wore a knee length Army coat in his rounds of Creede. He could actually make it billow and lift his neck hair when he was fully charged. Those old birds used to sit on the bench at Soapy's Pool Hall and Barber Shop and see who could lift himself highest with a single burst.</p>

  <p>I was on a job in Idaho. We were just backing out of a driveway in a Chevy Blazer when our Senior Geologist, Joe Kantor, ran out of our quarters and waved us to a stop. He stuck his head in the passenger side of the front seat with a topographic map in his hands to show us a new area we were to check. At that exact moment Denny Heyrman unloaded the product of three helpings of spaghetti that had been nine hours in fermentation. The blast reversed the arch of the springs on the vehicle and flattened two tires on his side. Mr. Kantor jerked his head backwards with such force he hit the top of the door and knocked himself unconscious. When David Jones and Denny Heyrman were "bursting with pride" it sounded like a concert of French Horns and Tubas.</p>

  <p>On another job, this time in Colorado, I was following two geologists who had dined on Mexican food the previous night with both refried beans and fresh Pinto variety supporting the enchiladas. We were traveling at a good clip and every mile or so we could see either the head of Bob Hawkins hanging out the left side or Dave Jones with his head out the right side of the vehicle. A young lady from Massachusetts was my assistant. She noticed the strange behavior of the pair and asked me what kind of game they were playing. I told her they were checking the rock units and were looking for younger age intrusives in the precambrian rocks.</p>

  <p>"Can they honestly identify the formations traveling so fast?"</p>

  <p>I assured her they could.</p>

  <p>"Gee," she went on, "it must take an awfully long time to become a real geologist."</p>

  <p>I wanted to tell her, "Only as long as it takes Mexican food to reach the colon."</p>

  <p>I had just returned to bed from an early morning bathroom visit when my bride severely violated our air space. I was clutching the bedding as tightly as I could to my neck and the mattress hoping for a brief respite to recover enough to open a window. My pulling on the covers awoke the dear lady whereupon she gasped several times and screamed, "Oh, ohhh, that is terrible," partially recovered she added, "if you ever do that again I'll go sleep on the couch."</p>

  <p>Like I advised the male faction several pages back, "Better try to get along with your Mother, she'll be the last female you will meet who will listen to your side of the story."</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>This is the last story in the book's table of contents; the text ends at the top of printed page 39, which is otherwise blank. The same subject, with the same pool-hall contest and an old Creede fellow named Clyde in a long coat, is argued in "Echoes from the Thundermug," in <a href="/writings/echoes-from-the-thundermug/married-life-and-mining-camps-part-two/#eat-beef-and-help-the-ozone-layer">"Eat Beef and Help the Ozone Layer."</a> The Clyde here is very likely Clyde "Unc" Hood, the railroad engineer in the old army overcoat of <a href="/writings/echoes-from-the-thundermug/grown-up-in-creede-part-one/#a-view-from-the-barroom-floor">"A View from the Barroom Floor,"</a> and the "Unc Hood" of the first story in this chapter; this story gives only "Clyde." Soapy's Pool Hall and Barber Shop is Fred "Soapy" Birdsey's shop, described in Echoes, in <a href="/writings/echoes-from-the-thundermug/creede-boyhood-part-one/#the-magic-age-is-five">"The Magic Age Is Five,"</a> and in "Once Around the Thunder Mug," <a href="/writings/once-around-the-thunder-mug/of-bagpipes-coasting-and-firemen/#lessons-from-a-barbers-chair">"Lessons from a Barber's Chair."</a> Joe Kantor, Bob Hawkins, Dave Jones and Denny Heyrman are the party crowd of Echoes; the first three are named together at a pig roast in <a href="/writings/echoes-from-the-thundermug/married-life-and-mining-camps-part-two/#rsvp">"RSVP,"</a> and Kantor is called "the geologist" in <a href="/writings/echoes-from-the-thundermug/married-life-and-mining-camps-part-four/#inez-hooks-the-big-one">"Inez Hooks the Big One."</a> The book does not say which mining company or year the Idaho job belongs to, and the young woman from Massachusetts is not named. "Genteel fahting," "generation of methane gas" and "David Jones" (against "Dave Jones" later) are as printed. The marriage advice in the last paragraph refers back to the end of "Let Me Put It Another Way," above.</em></p>
</section>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="one-more-clean-shirt-for-old-robert">One More Clean Shirt for Old Robert</h2>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>The book's last three pages carry no story heading and are not listed in the table of contents. This one is printed as page 40. The heading here is the photograph's caption.</em></p>

  <figure class="source-doc-image">
    <img src="/images/site/who-upset-old-robert-clean-shirt.jpg" alt="Black-and-white photograph of a white-haired figure with a lined face and few teeth, seated in a checked flannel shirt, white bib overalls and dark gloves, holding a pick across the lap, in a paneled room with a hat and other objects hanging on the wall behind.">
    <figcaption>One More Clean Shirt for Old Robert</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="source-doc-poem">
    <p>Sure I'm old and my lungs are scarred<br>
    but I'm headin' back to the lode<br>
    where tall pine trees have been standin' guard<br>
    and you live by the prospector's code</p>

    <p>Clean up your diggin's, take only your share<br>
    and help those whose luck went astray<br>
    faith is the beacon that leads us there<br>
    and gives us the strength for each day.</p>
  </div>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>The stanzas are printed as one unbroken verse in italics, with alternate lines indented; they are divided into two here at "code" / "Clean." The book does not say who "Old Robert" is or who is pictured. The face has the look of a mask or costume, but that is an impression from the page image, not something the book states.</em></p>
</section>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="victor-miller-and-noel-oates">Victor Miller and Noel Oates</h2>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>This page and the back cover that follows are unnumbered. The heading here is the photograph's caption. The caption and couplet on this page are set in a sans-serif face unlike the rest of the book.</em></p>

  <figure class="source-doc-image">
    <img src="/images/site/who-upset-victor-miller-noel-oates.jpg" alt="Black-and-white photograph of two men in hats standing side by side with their arms around each other, beside the open door of an old vehicle, in front of a brushy hillside. The man at left wears a miner's hat with a lamp, a dark cardigan and overalls; the man at right wears a plaid work shirt.">
    <figcaption>Victor Miller &amp; Noel Oates</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="source-doc-poem">
    <p>Vic was a mule-skinner in The Commodore Hole<br>
    where the ore was blasted by our good friend, Noel.</p>

    <p>I'll tell you, lad, it's a black old hole<br>
    when the flame flickers low and goes out<br>
    yuh curse the devil and pray for your soul<br>
    as pack rats scurry about</p>

    <p>Ain't nothin' good 'bout a hole in the ground<br>
    just takes yuh closer to hell<br>
    muckin' ore in a can and listen' for the sound<br>
    of the devil ringin' his bell</p>

    <p>I've been there lad, yep, worked with the best<br>
    in the tunnels and shafts at Creede<br>
    Alvie and Erick, Noel and the rest<br>
    we called them the last of the breed</p>

    <p>But they weren't the last and the list goes on<br>
    the pride of the next generation<br>
    taking the place of souls now long gone<br>
    all part of the good Lord's creation</p>
  </div>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>The photograph does not say which man is which. A "Vic Miller" is listed among the skinners of Emperius Mining Company in "Echoes from the Thundermug," in <a href="/writings/echoes-from-the-thundermug/grown-up-in-creede-part-one/#mule-skinners-of-40-years-ago-were-a-hardy-breed">"Mule Skinners of 40 Years Ago Were a Hardy Breed,"</a> which gives no first name; the match rests on the surname, the nickname "Vic" and the job. Echoes has Emperius Mining Company renovating the old Reynolds Tunnel, the Commodore 5 level, in the early 1940s ("Grown Up in Creede, Part One"). Noel Oates is not named elsewhere on the site, and nothing on the site connects him to the other Oateses in the Name Index. "Alvie" and "Erick" are given by first name only. Both names are on the site, as Alvie Wintz, in <a href="/writings/echoes-from-the-thundermug/married-life-and-mining-camps-part-three/#moves-keep-em-hopping">"Moves Keep 'Em Hopping,"</a> and as Erick W. Nelson in "Please Go to Hell, John Jackson," but the verse does not say these are the same men. "Listen' for the sound" is as printed.</em></p>
</section>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="about-the-author">About the Author</h2>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>The back cover carries a portrait and a paragraph, with no heading and no caption. The heading here is editorial. The portrait is presumably the author, as is usual on a back cover, but the book does not say so.</em></p>

  <figure class="source-doc-image">
    <img src="/images/site/who-upset-author-back-cover.jpg" alt="Black-and-white portrait of a smiling bearded man with short hair, in a collared shirt and vest, photographed against a wall of vertical wooden boards with deep shadow falling across the right side of the frame.">
  </figure>

  <p>John Richard Jackson had a working career, less military service, spanning a half century. He ran the gamut in professions from "Garbage Man" to part time columnist for a weekly newspaper, but his love was mining. His first literary effort, <em>Once Around The Thundermug,</em> chronicled the humorous side of growing up in the mining town of Creede, Colorado. <em>Who Upset the Thundermug,</em> continues in the same vein only more so. He believes we take life too seriously, that we should take a moment to laugh at ourselves. After all, our neighbors laugh at us...why should they have all the fun?</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>The back-cover text spells both titles "Thundermug" as one word; the front cover and title page have "Thunder Mug." The comma after the second title is as printed. John's own page is <a href="/people/john-richard-jackson/">John Richard Jackson</a>.</em></p>
</section>
